  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 203 PM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nThe southern plains upper low is exiting east into the southeast \nCONUS this afternoon, allowing upper ridge heights to amplify \nacross the Central Plains through Tuesday. Lighter winds  \nunder the influence of the sfc high in eastern Kansas may setup \nanother night of radiational fog along the river valleys. The fog \nshould lift quickly however as lee cyclogenesis deepens across the \nHigh Plains ahead of a Pacific trough entering the western CONUS. \nThe resulting tightening pressure gradient will drive breezy south \nwinds sustained from 15 to 25 mph, gusting up to 50 mph, highest \nbeing across central Kansas. Persistent southerly WAA boosts \ntemperatures well above mid-November normals in the middle to upper \n60s Tuesday through Friday.\n\nLate Wednesday evening into Thursday, a northern Plains surface low \nwill trail a weak frontal boundary through western portions of the \nforecast area, however deeper moisture return from the Gulf remains \nshunted south and east so overall precip chances are trending dry\nthrough Thursday afternoon.\n\nPrecipitation probabilities increase Friday afternoon into Saturday \nas ensemble consensus develops a stronger surface low east of the \nRockies and pushes the attendant cold front across Northeast Kansas, \nbringing more widespread showers and a few storms to the region. The\ntiming of the wave, trough amplification, and boundary-layer \nmoisture remain varied among ensemble clusters, despite operational \nmodels focusing higher QPF amounts further north towards Nebraska. \nOverall confidence in rainfall amounts therefore remains low. Behind \nthe front, a cold surface high settles in, bringing seasonably cold \nair Sunday into Monday with highs down to the upper 40s and lower \n50s.",
